WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:08.845
A transfer moves 25 euros from wallet A on page 0 to wallet B on page 132.

2
00:00:08.845 --> 00:00:14.575
The database changes these pages in memory, which is lost in a crash.

3
00:00:14.767 --> 00:00:17.826
Both changes must survive a crash, together.

4
00:00:17.826 --> 00:00:25.127
Saving both pages at commit means two 8 kilobyte writes in two places, and a force: about 4 milliseconds.

5
00:00:25.333 --> 00:00:29.863
And if the power fails between them, the disk holds the debit without the credit.

6
00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:33.699
So the database keeps a log, a file it only appends to.

7
00:00:33.699 --> 00:00:37.495
Before changing a page, it appends a record of the change.

8
00:00:37.667 --> 00:00:39.971
Both pages change only in memory.

9
00:00:39.971 --> 00:00:42.416
They're dirty: newer than the disk.

10
00:00:42.633 --> 00:00:49.997
At commit, it appends a commit record and forces the log to disk: one sequential write and one fsync.

11
00:00:49.997 --> 00:00:51.820
Then the client hears OK.

12
00:00:52.033 --> 00:00:55.773
The dirty pages go to the data file later, in the background.

13
00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:01.191
The writer starts on page 132, and halfway through, the power fails.

14
00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:02.706
Memory is gone.

15
00:01:02.706 --> 00:01:05.754
Page 0 on disk has the old balance.

16
00:01:05.754 --> 00:01:12.023
Page 132 is torn: a drive writes 4 kilobytes at a time, and a page is 8.

17
00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:15.491
Recovery redoes changes from the last checkpoint on.

18
00:01:15.491 --> 00:01:20.555
The checkpoint wrote every dirty page to disk, so nothing older needs replaying.

19
00:01:20.767 --> 00:01:23.673
It replays each record onto its page.

20
00:01:23.673 --> 00:01:25.793
Page 0 gets its debit back.

21
00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:29.407
But a small record assumes an intact page.

22
00:01:29.407 --> 00:01:32.733
On torn page 132, it would write garbage.

23
00:01:32.933 --> 00:01:39.613
So the first change to a page after a checkpoint logs a full copy of it, a full-page image.

24
00:01:39.613 --> 00:01:42.696
Recovery restores page 132 from that copy.

25
00:01:42.867 --> 00:01:46.905
T1's commit record is there, so both changes are back, together.

26
00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:49.037
A commit forces only the log.

27
00:01:49.037 --> 00:01:53.577
The data file can lag, or even tear, because the log can rebuild it.
